Choosing where to open a business, or judging whether a neighbourhood is safe, well connected and affordable, normally means weeks of manual surveys across maps, listings and reports. Geographic Information Systems can answer such questions but demand specialist skills, and large language models (LLMs) that answer directly tend to invent place names and coordinates, so their output cannot be verified.

This project presents a natural-language geospatial query and decision-support system built on a spatial database. A user types a question in plain English, such as "hospitals within 1 km of a metro station". An LLM writes a PostGIS query against a documented description of the schema; the query is validated and run under a read-only database role, and if it fails or returns nothing the model is asked, within fixed limits, for a corrected query. The result is an interactive map layer with a short explanation and the exact SQL, so every answer can be checked.

The system uses PostgreSQL with PostGIS, Martin vector tiles, a FastAPI backend and a React frontend. A loading pipeline built on OpenStreetMap fills the database for any city; the deployed instance holds Mumbai, with its boundaries, roads, transit stops, about 10,800 businesses and points of interest, a 76,000-row house-price dataset and area-level safety scores. Four decision tools use the same data: a site report, a competition and catchment analysis, a cost-to-open and break-even estimator, and a comparison of up to three locations. Measured values are labelled separately from modelled estimates (rent, revenue and payback).

A benchmark of 39 spatial questions with hand-written gold queries shows that the engine returns exactly the gold answer for 74.4% of them (geometry F1 88.4%) and that every generated query ran; most failures come from a row cap and the strictness of exact-match scoring. A direct-LLM baseline that names places and coordinates without a database returned names that exist in the database only 19.5% of the time. The limitations are stated openly: a small single-run benchmark, no real rental data, heuristic demand and revenue models, and listing locations accurate only to the region. Having an LLM write a query that is executed on real data gives answers that are both useful and verifiable.

*Keywords:* natural-language interface, geospatial query, text-to-SQL, PostGIS, large language models, site selection
